1
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:07,032
Three weeks ago, at exactly eight seventeen in the morning,

2
00:00:07,072 --> 00:00:09,880
every human being on Earth disappeared.

3
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,805
Every human being... except one.

4
00:00:14,905 --> 00:00:17,983
Noah spent ten days alone in an empty city.

5
00:00:18,023 --> 00:00:22,333
Then a radio led him to an old observatory in the mountains,

6
00:00:22,373 --> 00:00:26,583
and to the recordings of a scientist named Elena Carter.

7
00:00:27,683 --> 00:00:29,670
The disappearance... wasn't random.

8
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:35,318
He learned that a mysterious signal had been coming from Earth itself.

9
00:00:35,358 --> 00:00:37,678
He heard his missing sister's voice.

10
00:00:37,718 --> 00:00:42,259
And he watched a ring of light form in the sky above the mountains.

11
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:48,052
Then every screen in the observatory displayed the same message.

12
00:00:49,152 --> 00:00:50,792
Phase two begins tomorrow.

13
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:54,488
This is what happened... the next day.

14
00:01:04,088 --> 00:01:08,723
Noah woke on the floor of the observatory, curled up beside a dead radio,

15
00:01:08,763 --> 00:01:13,011
with the first light of morning coming through the high windows.

16
00:01:14,111 --> 00:01:17,148
For a moment, he didn't remember where he was.

17
00:01:17,188 --> 00:01:19,879
Then he remembered everything at once.

18
00:01:20,979 --> 00:01:23,686
The power had come back during the night.

19
00:01:23,726 --> 00:01:27,371
One by one, every screen in the room flickered to life.

20
00:01:27,411 --> 00:01:30,777
And on every one of them, a single line of text.

21
00:01:31,877 --> 00:01:35,014
Phase two. Then a number. Counting down.

22
00:01:36,114 --> 00:01:37,187
Twenty-four hours.

23
00:01:37,227 --> 00:01:40,715
Something is happening in twenty-four hours. But what?

24
00:01:41,815 --> 00:01:43,483
A darker thought crept in.

25
00:01:43,523 --> 00:01:46,900
What if phase two was not a return... but an ending?

26
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:51,106
What if whatever had taken eight billion people was coming back,

27
00:01:51,146 --> 00:01:53,070
this time for the last one?

28
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:55,226
He went outside.

29
00:01:55,266 --> 00:01:57,144
The ring of lights was gone.

30
00:01:57,184 --> 00:02:01,117
The sky above the mountains was clear, blue, and ordinary.

31
00:02:01,157 --> 00:02:03,636
As if nothing had happened at all.

32
00:02:04,736 --> 00:02:09,408
But something was different. The birds were silent. Not one was singing.

33
00:02:09,448 --> 00:02:14,965
And the wind carried a strange metallic smell, like the air before a thunderstorm.

34
00:02:16,065 --> 00:02:17,759
Noah went back inside.

35
00:02:17,799 --> 00:02:22,756
If the answer existed anywhere, it was in Dr. Carter's files.

36
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:29,456
On her desk, beside a cold cup of coffee, he found a page of handwritten notes.

37
00:02:29,496 --> 00:02:30,670
Equations. Dates.

38
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:36,381
And one sentence, written in the margin and circled twice. It's not taking them.

39
00:02:36,421 --> 00:02:37,785
It's hiding them.

40
00:02:42,885 --> 00:02:46,288
He had watched every recording on the main computer.

41
00:02:46,328 --> 00:02:50,790
But there was one folder he hadn't been able to open. It was locked.

42
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:52,701
And it had a strange name.

43
00:02:53,801 --> 00:02:56,719
The password hint was a single question.

44
00:02:56,759 --> 00:02:59,127
What did the signal say first?

45
00:03:00,227 --> 00:03:02,759
Noah thought about it for a long time.

46
00:03:02,799 --> 00:03:06,413
Then he remembered the radio in the electronics store.

47
00:03:06,453 --> 00:03:10,731
The very first words he had heard, after ten days of silence.

48
00:03:11,831 --> 00:03:17,500
He tried everything. Restoration. Disconnected. Phase two. Look at the sky.

49
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:22,334
Every attempt ended with the same red message. Access denied.

50
00:03:23,434 --> 00:03:25,773
...is anyone receiving this transmission...?

51
00:03:26,873 --> 00:03:31,487
He typed them in, one letter at a time. Is anyone receiving.

52
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:34,326
The folder opened.

53
00:03:35,426 --> 00:03:37,634
Inside was a single video file.

54
00:03:37,674 --> 00:03:40,607
Recorded at eight sixteen in the morning.

55
00:03:40,647 --> 00:03:43,407
One minute before the disappearance.

56
00:03:44,507 --> 00:03:48,501
If you're watching this, then you're the one who stayed. I'm sorry. I'm so sorry.

57
00:03:48,541 --> 00:03:49,496
I didn't choose you.

58
00:03:49,536 --> 00:03:52,973
But I think I understand why it happened. And you deserve to know.

59
00:03:54,073 --> 00:03:56,097
Dr. Carter looked exhausted.

60
00:03:56,137 --> 00:04:02,287
Behind her, a window showed the same mountains Noah could see outside. The same sky.

61
00:04:02,327 --> 00:04:03,951
Three weeks earlier.

62
00:04:05,051 --> 00:04:06,255
I have a son. He's nine.

63
00:04:06,295 --> 00:04:08,225
He's at school right now, in the city.

64
00:04:08,265 --> 00:04:11,594
I keep telling myself that wherever they're going, he'll be safe.

65
00:04:11,634 --> 00:04:12,786
I have to believe that.

66
00:04:12,826 --> 00:04:14,479
Otherwise I couldn't do this.

67
00:04:19,579 --> 00:04:24,567
The signal we found isn't a message from aliens. It isn't a weapon. It's an alarm.

68
00:04:24,607 --> 00:04:26,167
A very, very old alarm.

69
00:04:27,267 --> 00:04:27,422
Dr.

70
00:04:27,462 --> 00:04:31,136
Carter's team had traced the signal to the planet's core.

71
00:04:31,176 --> 00:04:33,286
Somewhere deep beneath the crust,

72
00:04:33,326 --> 00:04:38,173
something had been listening to the Sun for longer than humans had existed.

73
00:04:38,213 --> 00:04:42,663
And eleven days before the disappearance, it had started to count.

74
00:04:43,763 --> 00:04:47,765
We checked the solar observatories. All of them. There is a flare coming.

75
00:04:47,805 --> 00:04:50,036
Bigger than anything in recorded history.

76
00:04:50,076 --> 00:04:54,576
Big enough to strip the atmosphere off the day side of the planet for a few hours.

77
00:04:54,616 --> 00:04:57,258
Nothing exposed on the surface would survive.

78
00:04:58,358 --> 00:05:03,531
Noah stopped the video. He played that part again. And then a third time.

79
00:05:04,631 --> 00:05:08,729
He went to the main telescope, fitted the solar filter Dr.

80
00:05:08,769 --> 00:05:12,510
Carter had left beside it, and pointed it at the Sun.

81
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:14,722
On its surface was a dark scar,

82
00:05:14,762 --> 00:05:20,834
a sunspot so enormous that the whole Earth could have fallen into it a dozen times.

83
00:05:21,934 --> 00:05:26,279
I think whatever is down there was built to protect life on this planet. All of it.

84
00:05:26,319 --> 00:05:30,294
And at eight seventeen, it's going to move us. Somewhere safe. Out of phase.

85
00:05:30,334 --> 00:05:32,491
Out of time. Temporarily disconnected.

86
00:05:33,591 --> 00:05:35,529
Humanity hadn't been taken.

87
00:05:35,569 --> 00:05:37,580
Humanity had been evacuated.

88
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:42,458
Hidden, the way you might lift a child out of the path of a car.

89
00:05:43,558 --> 00:05:47,351
If my calculations are right, when it's over, it will put everyone back.

90
00:05:47,391 --> 00:05:49,906
Exactly where they were. Exactly when they were.

91
00:05:49,946 --> 00:05:52,757
For us, it will feel like nothing happened at all.

92
00:05:53,857 --> 00:05:55,772
But I don't understand one thing.

93
00:05:55,812 --> 00:05:59,267
In every simulation, it leaves one person behind. Just one.

94
00:05:59,307 --> 00:06:01,767
As if someone has to stay... and watch.

95
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:04,700
The video ended.

96
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,570
Noah looked at the countdown on the screen beside him.

97
00:06:09,610 --> 00:06:14,558
Phase two wasn't the end of the evacuation. Phase two was the storm.

98
00:06:19,658 --> 00:06:19,830
Dr.

99
00:06:19,870 --> 00:06:23,499
Carter had left instructions at the end of the file.

100
00:06:23,539 --> 00:06:25,686
The observatory had a basement,

101
00:06:25,726 --> 00:06:30,555
built in the nineteen sixties to protect the telescopes' instruments.

102
00:06:30,595 --> 00:06:33,074
Thick concrete. Deep underground.

103
00:06:34,174 --> 00:06:40,017
Noah filled it with everything he could carry. Water. Blankets. Food. The radio.

104
00:06:40,057 --> 00:06:41,604
And then he waited.

105
00:06:42,704 --> 00:06:47,124
If anyone ever hears this. It's the last day before... whatever this is.

106
00:06:47,164 --> 00:06:52,269
I'm not scared. That's a lie. I'm terrified. But I'm still here. I'm still here.

107
00:06:53,369 --> 00:06:58,212
That evening, he watched what might be the last sunset of the old world,

108
00:06:58,252 --> 00:07:00,925
sitting on the steps of the observatory.

109
00:07:00,965 --> 00:07:03,841
The sky turned gold, then red, then purple.

110
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:07,626
The first stars came out over the valley, one by one.

111
00:07:08,726 --> 00:07:12,844
Then he wrote a letter to his sister, on the back of one of Dr.

112
00:07:12,884 --> 00:07:14,230
Carter's star charts.

113
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:17,925
He wrote about the empty city, the radio, the mountains.

114
00:07:17,965 --> 00:07:21,159
He wrote that he was sorry he hadn't called back.

115
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:24,781
He folded it into his jacket pocket, over his heart.

116
00:07:25,881 --> 00:07:28,522
At two minutes past eight the next morning,

117
00:07:28,562 --> 00:07:31,954
he climbed the stairs one last time to look outside.

118
00:07:33,054 --> 00:07:34,858
The sky was changing color.

119
00:07:34,898 --> 00:07:39,367
The blue drained away to white, then to a deep, shimmering violet.

120
00:07:39,407 --> 00:07:43,656
The sun was too bright to look at, even through closed eyes.

121
00:07:44,756 --> 00:07:49,348
And then, across the entire sky, the aurora appeared. Not at night.

122
00:07:49,388 --> 00:07:51,076
In the middle of the day.

123
00:07:51,116 --> 00:07:56,192
Curtains of red and green and violet light, from one horizon to the other,

124
00:07:56,232 --> 00:07:58,802
rippling like an ocean upside down.

125
00:07:59,902 --> 00:08:03,235
Every electrical line in the valley began to hum.

126
00:08:03,275 --> 00:08:07,625
Sparks jumped from the metal railings. His hair stood on end.

127
00:08:08,725 --> 00:08:12,859
Noah ran down the stairs, slammed the basement door behind him,

128
00:08:12,899 --> 00:08:15,368
and spun the wheel until it locked.

129
00:08:22,868 --> 00:08:26,479
What happened on the surface over the next four hours,

130
00:08:26,519 --> 00:08:31,132
Noah would only ever see from the observatory's automatic cameras.

131
00:08:32,232 --> 00:08:35,823
A wave of solar fire, larger than the Earth itself,

132
00:08:35,863 --> 00:08:41,732
erupted from the Sun and crossed a hundred and fifty million kilometers of space in

133
00:08:41,772 --> 00:08:43,061
less than a day.

134
00:08:44,161 --> 00:08:49,392
And as it reached the planet, the ring appeared again. Not one ring this time.

135
00:08:49,432 --> 00:08:50,608
Thousands of them.

136
00:08:50,648 --> 00:08:56,136
Glowing lines of light, wrapping the entire Earth like the threads of a cocoon.

137
00:08:57,236 --> 00:08:59,062
The storm hit the shield...

138
00:08:59,102 --> 00:09:03,190
and bent around it, the way a river bends around a stone.

139
00:09:04,290 --> 00:09:08,055
In the basement, the lights flickered. The floor trembled.

140
00:09:08,095 --> 00:09:10,810
A deep, low hum came through the concrete,

141
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:13,821
so powerful that Noah felt it in his teeth.

142
00:09:14,921 --> 00:09:19,834
He sat with his back against the wall and talked to his sister. Out loud.

143
00:09:19,874 --> 00:09:20,987
About everything.

144
00:09:21,027 --> 00:09:26,740
About their parents' rooftop, and the stars, and the phone call he never returned.

145
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:31,148
He wondered where they all were, right at that moment.

146
00:09:31,188 --> 00:09:35,797
Eight billion people, hidden somewhere outside of time. Were they dreaming?

147
00:09:35,837 --> 00:09:37,657
Did they feel anything at all?

148
00:09:37,697 --> 00:09:41,443
Or were they simply paused, like a song between two notes?

149
00:09:42,543 --> 00:09:45,967
And then, after four hours... the hum stopped.

150
00:09:50,067 --> 00:09:55,395
When Noah opened the door and climbed into the daylight, the sky was blue again.

151
00:09:55,435 --> 00:09:56,670
Birds were singing.

152
00:09:56,710 --> 00:10:01,356
The forest on the mountainside was green, and whole, and untouched.

153
00:10:02,456 --> 00:10:07,718
On every screen inside, the countdown had been replaced by a new message.

154
00:10:12,818 --> 00:10:15,725
Noah drove back to the city through the night,

155
00:10:15,765 --> 00:10:20,080
in a car he had found by the side of the road. He wanted to be home.

156
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:23,473
Whatever happened next, he wanted to see it there.

157
00:10:24,573 --> 00:10:28,144
He drove slowly through the empty streets one last time.

158
00:10:28,184 --> 00:10:32,593
Past the supermarket with the music still playing. Past the hospital.

159
00:10:32,633 --> 00:10:36,462
Past the electronics store where the radio had first spoken.

160
00:10:36,502 --> 00:10:41,750
He realized, with surprise, that a small part of him would miss this. The silence.

161
00:10:41,790 --> 00:10:42,394
The stars.

162
00:10:42,434 --> 00:10:45,744
The feeling of having the whole world to himself.

163
00:10:46,844 --> 00:10:51,589
At eight o'clock the next morning, he stood in the middle of the main avenue,

164
00:10:51,629 --> 00:10:54,638
where he had stood alone so many times before.

165
00:10:55,738 --> 00:10:58,337
Eight fifteen. Eight sixteen.

166
00:10:59,437 --> 00:11:04,831
His hands were shaking. What if it didn't work? What if Dr. Carter had been wrong?

167
00:11:04,871 --> 00:11:08,344
What if, at eight seventeen, nothing happened at all,

168
00:11:08,384 --> 00:11:11,450
and he stayed alone in this silence forever?

169
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:16,148
At eight seventeen, the air in front of him shimmered,

170
00:11:16,188 --> 00:11:18,561
like heat above a road in summer.

171
00:11:19,661 --> 00:11:22,481
And then, all at once, they were there.

172
00:11:26,581 --> 00:11:31,205
Thousands of people. Mid-step. Mid-sentence. A man finishing a yawn.

173
00:11:31,245 --> 00:11:33,400
A woman laughing into her phone.

174
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:37,241
A child reaching for a pigeon that, three weeks earlier,

175
00:11:37,281 --> 00:11:39,900
had been sitting on that exact spot.

176
00:11:40,999 --> 00:11:46,420
Cars that had stood still for twenty-one days lurched forward. Horns sounded.

177
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:48,689
A bus pulled away from its stop.

178
00:11:48,729 --> 00:11:52,283
The city roared back to life in a single breath.

179
00:11:53,383 --> 00:11:57,877
Noah stood in the middle of the crowd, and he couldn't stop crying.

180
00:11:57,917 --> 00:12:00,516
People walked around him. A few stared.

181
00:12:00,556 --> 00:12:02,669
A woman asked if he was okay.

182
00:12:03,769 --> 00:12:05,765
You're back. You're all back. You're back.

183
00:12:10,865 --> 00:12:15,914
But nobody else understood what had happened. Because for them... nothing had.

184
00:12:17,014 --> 00:12:21,820
For eight billion people, the disappearance had lasted less than a second.

185
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:25,290
A strange shiver in the air. A flicker of the lights.

186
00:12:25,330 --> 00:12:29,344
A moment of dizziness, gone before they could even name it.

187
00:12:30,444 --> 00:12:36,202
Their phones, connected to the network again, insisted that three weeks had passed.

188
00:12:36,242 --> 00:12:41,371
Scientists on television argued about clock errors and satellite glitches.

189
00:12:41,411 --> 00:12:43,238
Nobody could explain it.

190
00:12:44,338 --> 00:12:45,981
And the plants were taller.

191
00:12:46,021 --> 00:12:48,973
And the food in their refrigerators had spoiled.

192
00:12:49,013 --> 00:12:53,962
And the streets were covered in leaves that hadn't been there the day before.

193
00:12:55,062 --> 00:13:00,688
In Noah's apartment, everything was exactly as he had left it. Except for one thing.

194
00:13:00,728 --> 00:13:02,982
The small plant on his windowsill,

195
00:13:03,022 --> 00:13:08,568
the one he had watered every single day for three weeks, had grown a new flower.

196
00:13:09,668 --> 00:13:14,962
Noah tried to tell people the truth. A neighbor. A police officer. A reporter.

197
00:13:15,002 --> 00:13:16,535
They listened politely,

198
00:13:16,575 --> 00:13:22,142
and then they looked at him the way you look at someone who hasn't slept in a very

199
00:13:22,182 --> 00:13:23,016
long time.

200
00:13:24,116 --> 00:13:26,762
His boss called that afternoon, furious,

201
00:13:26,802 --> 00:13:30,388
asking why he hadn't shown up for work in three weeks.

202
00:13:30,428 --> 00:13:33,935
Noah laughed so hard that his boss hung up on him.

203
00:13:35,035 --> 00:13:39,254
I was alone. For three weeks. The whole planet. I walked to the mountains.

204
00:13:39,294 --> 00:13:41,228
I saw the storm. I'm not crazy.

205
00:13:42,328 --> 00:13:46,291
And maybe the hardest part of being the last human on Earth...

206
00:13:46,331 --> 00:13:49,128
was being the only one who remembered it.

207
00:13:54,228 --> 00:13:58,042
There was only one person Noah truly needed to see.

208
00:13:59,142 --> 00:14:04,559
His sister, Mia, lived across the river, in a small apartment with a red door.

209
00:14:04,599 --> 00:14:08,876
He ran the whole way. Fourteen blocks. He didn't stop once.

210
00:14:09,976 --> 00:14:14,701
She opened the door in her pajamas, with a cup of coffee in her hand,

211
00:14:14,741 --> 00:14:17,170
and a confused smile on her face.

212
00:14:18,270 --> 00:14:22,022
Noah? What are you doing here? It's eight in the morning. You look terrible.

213
00:14:22,062 --> 00:14:22,861
Are you okay?

214
00:14:23,961 --> 00:14:26,383
He didn't answer. He just hugged her.

215
00:14:26,423 --> 00:14:29,102
For so long that her coffee went cold.

216
00:14:30,202 --> 00:14:34,273
Then he took a folded piece of paper out of his jacket pocket,

217
00:14:34,313 --> 00:14:37,788
the back of a star chart, covered in his handwriting,

218
00:14:37,828 --> 00:14:40,232
and gave it to her without a word.

219
00:14:41,332 --> 00:14:42,448
Mia read it twice.

220
00:14:42,488 --> 00:14:44,887
When she looked up, her eyes were wet.

221
00:14:44,927 --> 00:14:48,929
She didn't understand most of it. But she understood enough.

222
00:14:50,029 --> 00:14:54,480
Later, sitting at her kitchen table, she finally gave him her news.

223
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:58,557
The reason for the message. Call me tomorrow. I have news.

224
00:14:59,657 --> 00:15:00,980
I'm going to have a baby.

225
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:05,040
You're going to be an uncle. I wanted to tell you first. Before anyone.

226
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:09,872
Noah laughed and cried at the same time. Again.

227
00:15:10,972 --> 00:15:14,434
Then Mia went quiet, and looked at him strangely.

228
00:15:15,534 --> 00:15:17,334
This is going to sound crazy.

229
00:15:17,374 --> 00:15:21,713
Last night I had the longest dream. I was calling you. Over and over.

230
00:15:21,753 --> 00:15:27,106
On a radio, I think. And you couldn't hear me. I kept telling you... look outside.

231
00:15:28,206 --> 00:15:29,416
Noah said nothing.

232
00:15:29,456 --> 00:15:33,077
He just held his sister's hand, and didn't let go.

233
00:15:38,177 --> 00:15:41,680
A week later, Noah drove back to the mountains.

234
00:15:42,780 --> 00:15:45,574
The observatory was full of people again.

235
00:15:45,614 --> 00:15:47,925
Scientists, technicians, students.

236
00:15:47,965 --> 00:15:52,349
Nobody knew why the main computer's clock was three weeks ahead,

237
00:15:52,389 --> 00:15:56,480
or why the basement was full of blankets and canned food.

238
00:15:57,580 --> 00:15:59,629
And there, in the control room,

239
00:15:59,669 --> 00:16:04,077
was a tired-looking woman with grey eyes and her hair pulled back.

240
00:16:04,117 --> 00:16:07,300
Exactly as he remembered her from the screen.

241
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:10,966
Can I help you? This area is restricted.

242
00:16:12,066 --> 00:16:17,004
Dr. Carter. My name is Noah. You don't know me. But you left me a message.

243
00:16:18,104 --> 00:16:21,094
She didn't believe him. Of course she didn't.

244
00:16:21,134 --> 00:16:25,931
Not until he asked her to open a folder called For the one who stays.

245
00:16:27,031 --> 00:16:32,238
She watched her own face on the screen, saying words she didn't remember saying.

246
00:16:32,278 --> 00:16:36,166
When the video ended, she sat in silence for a long time.

247
00:16:37,266 --> 00:16:40,964
I don't remember recording this. But that's my voice. Those are my words.

248
00:16:41,004 --> 00:16:43,356
And that is exactly what I would have said.

249
00:16:44,456 --> 00:16:46,431
Should we tell people? Everyone?

250
00:16:47,531 --> 00:16:48,270
Tell them what?

251
00:16:48,310 --> 00:16:52,164
That something under our feet has been protecting us for millions of years,

252
00:16:52,204 --> 00:16:54,864
and nobody noticed? They'll laugh. Or they'll panic.

253
00:16:54,904 --> 00:16:58,844
Or they'll try to dig it up. No. First, we understand it. Then we decide.

254
00:16:59,944 --> 00:17:04,785
Together, they pulled the data from every solar observatory on the planet.

255
00:17:04,825 --> 00:17:05,907
It was all there.

256
00:17:05,947 --> 00:17:08,743
A flare that should have been catastrophic.

257
00:17:08,783 --> 00:17:10,656
And at the exact same moment,

258
00:17:10,696 --> 00:17:15,398
a shield of energy around the Earth that no instrument could explain.

259
00:17:20,498 --> 00:17:23,690
There was still one question left. The one Dr.

260
00:17:23,730 --> 00:17:25,798
Carter had asked in her video.

261
00:17:25,838 --> 00:17:27,956
Why leave one person behind?

262
00:17:29,056 --> 00:17:34,547
That night, alone in the control room, Noah turned on the old radio one last time,

263
00:17:34,587 --> 00:17:38,784
and turned the dial to the frequency where it had all begun.

264
00:17:42,884 --> 00:17:48,600
For a long time, there was only static. And then a voice. Not his sister's. Not Dr.

265
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:49,224
Carter's.

266
00:17:49,264 --> 00:17:54,060
A voice that seemed to be made of all the voices he had ever heard.

267
00:17:55,160 --> 00:17:56,569
Observer. Thank you.

268
00:17:57,669 --> 00:17:58,061
Why me?

269
00:17:58,101 --> 00:17:59,981
Why did you leave me behind?

270
00:18:01,081 --> 00:18:02,372
Someone must remain.

271
00:18:02,412 --> 00:18:05,168
Someone must see that the world continues.

272
00:18:05,208 --> 00:18:08,687
Someone must decide that it is worth returning to.

273
00:18:09,787 --> 00:18:11,141
Will it happen again?

274
00:18:12,241 --> 00:18:13,267
Only if it must.

275
00:18:13,307 --> 00:18:15,865
We have been here for a very long time.

276
00:18:15,905 --> 00:18:18,920
We will be here for a very long time after.

277
00:18:20,020 --> 00:18:23,748
Noah thought about the empty city. The silent streets.

278
00:18:23,788 --> 00:18:25,772
The stars over the mountains.

279
00:18:25,812 --> 00:18:28,284
The fox and the deer on the highway.

280
00:18:28,324 --> 00:18:32,800
The world, alive and beautiful, even without anyone to see it.

281
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:36,296
It is. It's worth it. Every part of it.

282
00:18:37,396 --> 00:18:38,920
The radio fell silent.

283
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:44,298
On the screen beside it, a final message appeared... and then faded away.

284
00:18:49,398 --> 00:18:52,822
Noah never told the story publicly. Only Mia, Dr.

285
00:18:52,862 --> 00:18:58,478
Carter, and a handful of scientists know what really happened during those three

286
00:18:58,518 --> 00:18:59,092
weeks.

287
00:19:00,192 --> 00:19:02,768
Five months later, Mia had a baby girl.

288
00:19:02,808 --> 00:19:05,709
She asked Noah to choose her middle name.

289
00:19:06,809 --> 00:19:10,197
Every year, on the anniversary of the disappearance,

290
00:19:10,237 --> 00:19:13,163
Noah drives up to the observatory. He and Dr.

291
00:19:13,203 --> 00:19:17,513
Carter sit on the steps with two cups of coffee and watch the sky.

292
00:19:17,553 --> 00:19:20,406
They don't talk much. They don't need to.

293
00:19:21,506 --> 00:19:23,443
He chose Aurora.

294
00:19:24,543 --> 00:19:27,037
On clear nights, when she was old enough,

295
00:19:27,077 --> 00:19:30,499
he would take her up to the roof and show her the stars.

296
00:19:30,539 --> 00:19:33,713
The same ones Mia had shown him when they were kids.

297
00:19:33,753 --> 00:19:38,786
And he would tell her that the sky is always there. Even when you can't see it.

298
00:19:39,886 --> 00:19:45,222
Sometimes, early in the morning, Noah walks through the city before it wakes up.

299
00:19:45,262 --> 00:19:47,898
The streets are almost empty. Almost.

300
00:19:48,998 --> 00:19:54,517
And he listens to the sounds that most people never notice. Footsteps. Voices.

301
00:19:54,557 --> 00:19:56,085
A bus in the distance.

302
00:19:56,125 --> 00:19:58,508
A neighbor's radio through a wall.

303
00:19:58,548 --> 00:20:02,547
The noise of eight billion people, living their lives.

304
00:20:03,647 --> 00:20:07,020
It's the most beautiful sound he has ever heard.

305
00:20:08,120 --> 00:20:11,913
Because he knows what the world sounds like... without it.

306
00:20:18,013 --> 00:20:21,966
If you were the only person who remembered something like this...

307
00:20:22,006 --> 00:20:23,318
would you tell anyone?

308
00:20:23,358 --> 00:20:27,071
Or would you keep it to yourself? Tell us in the comments.

309
00:20:28,171 --> 00:20:29,215
And remember...

310
00:20:29,255 --> 00:20:32,223
when everything ends, the story begins.
